# NULLSPIN — Printables listing copy

> Generated from the gate suite. Every number below was measured by the run
> that wrote this file. **Do not hand-edit numbers here** — edit the campaign
> and re-run. Authorship is disclosed in full under "How this model was made".

---

## Title

**NULLSPIN — the fully-printed spinner whose two rims turn opposite ways (exact 7:11)**

## Summary

Flick the outer ring. The inner puck turns the **other way**, at an exact
integer ratio: **flick the ring 7 times and the puck turns 11 times back**
(7 × 66 teeth = 11 × 42 teeth = 462, exactly).

Because the two rotors turn in opposite directions, their angular momenta very
nearly cancel — **{eta_pct:.1}% of the spin angular momentum is cancelled** — so
the spinner barely resists being tilted. A second puck is included that is
deliberately NOT cancelled, so you can feel the difference yourself in twenty
seconds. That A/B is the model.

**You also need: nothing.** No bearing, no balls, no magnets, no screws, no
nuts, no weights, no inserts, no glue, no tools. Six printed parts.

Ø{od:.1} × {height:.2} mm · {printed_g:.1} g of PLA · no supports.

## Description (TOC: Components / Materials / Testing / Print settings / Assembly)

### Components

A grounded-carrier ("star") epicyclic gear train. The frame you hold IS the
planet carrier, so the six planet axes are FIXED — they never orbit. That is
unusual, and it is the whole design:

- **base spider** — the held frame: hub, six arms, six planet pins, the centre
  post the sun turns on, and six thrust pads the ring rides on
- **ring gear, 66T** — the outer rotor, the one you flick
- **sun puck, 42T** — the inner rotor, turning the other way, running directly
  on the printed post
- **six planets, 12T** — on fixed pins, coupling the two
- **top spider** — a 7 deg BAYONET onto the six pins, captures everything, twists
  off for the A/B
- **cap** — the static thumb pad, and what keeps the sun in when you invert it

Published in full, because the most-downloaded gear spinner on this site
publishes none of it:

| | |
|---|---|
| module · pressure angle | 1.0 mm · 25° |
| teeth (sun / planet / ring) | 42 / 12 / 66, six planets |
| centre distance | 27.000 mm, identical at both meshes |
| exact ratio, ring → sun | −11/7 = −1.5714 (counter-rotating) |
| exact ratio, ring → planet | +11/2 = +5.5 |
| contact ratio, sun–planet | {eps_sp:.3} |
| contact ratio, planet–ring | {eps_pr:.3} |
| circular backlash per mesh | {jt:.3} mm, measured by bisection on the CAD |
| tip/root clearance | 0.250 mm (0.25·m, ISO 53) at all four flanks |
| adjacent-planet gap | {neighbour:.2} mm |
| tooth-tip chamfer | 0.30 × 45°, both faces, every gear |
| ring mass · I_z | {mg_r:.2} g · {izz_r:.0} g·mm² |
| sun mass · I_z | {mg_s:.2} g · {izz_s:.0} g·mm² |
| planet mass, each | {mg_p:.2} g |
| effective inertia at the ring | {i_eff:.0} g·mm² |
| static imbalance | 0.000 g·mm, every rotor |
| tooth-passing frequency | 660 Hz at 600 rpm, both meshes (kinematic only — no loudness is claimed) |

### Materials

**You also need: nothing.**

That is the complete list. This model has no non-printed parts of any kind —
no bearing, no balls, no magnets, no screws, no nuts, no weights, no inserts,
no glue, no tools. {printed_g:.1} g of PLA and you are done.

Earlier versions of this design were not like that: v1 used one 608 bearing and
v2 added 24 loose Ø1.50 mm chrome-steel balls as a thrust race under the ring.
Deleting both was a deliberate choice and **it cost spin time**, which the
Testing section quantifies rather than glosses.

### Testing

Everything here is generated by a gate suite that re-proves every claim on
every build and refuses to produce files if any check fails. What was verified,
and what was NOT:

**Verified in CAD:** the exact ratio (as integer identities, plus the kernel's
own epicyclic assembly conditions); both contact ratios; tip and root clearance
at all four flanks; the undercut floor; interference over one complete mesh
cycle and two full revolutions with all six planets — with a negative control
that drives the sun 5% off ratio and confirms it jams; backlash by bisection to
flank contact; static and dynamic balance of every rotor; support-free
printability of every part; and that every part is a single connected body.

**Verified as GEOMETRY, not as a fit — the cover that holds it all in.** A
cover that falls off drops six planets on the floor, so the joint that holds it
is gated harder than anything else here. It is a bayonet: {engage:.2} mm of
solid pin fin sitting over the slot wall once you twist it home, with nothing
strained at rest. Earlier versions of this model used a press fit, and the
honest problem with a press fit at this scale is that its whole grip is
0.025 mm of interference — a printer running 0.15 mm oversize per side, which
is the worst case this design sizes every other clearance against, erases it
completely and the cover becomes a slip fit. The bayonet cannot do that: rebuild
BOTH parts with 0.35 mm/side of error against each other and there is still
{efull:.2} mm of shoulder left. The proof runs on the actual solids — lift the
assembled cover and it must collide with the pins; delete the slot's lip, or
pose the cover untwisted, and the same measurement must go to exactly zero.

A snap-fit was considered and is refused in writing, with the arithmetic: a
Ø5.60 hole in this arm can stretch {snapmax:.3} mm before PLA yields, and the
printer error a snap would have to swallow is 0.30 mm. That is {snapratio:.1}x
short, and no version of it fits inside a 12 mm-tall toy.

**Modelled, not measured — the angular-momentum cancellation.** eta =
{eta:.4}, and across a ±5% print-flow sensitivity sweep the worst corner is
{eta_lo:.4}. The control puck is {eta_b:.4} on purpose. **There is no
instrument for measuring this on a printed part, here or anywhere in the
hobby**, so it is published as a modelled band with its sensitivity table and
never as a single headline percentage. What you CAN do is feel it: swap the two
pucks and tilt.

One thing got *better* when the bearing left: with no steel in the rotor set,
eta is a pure ratio of PLA inertias, so a print-flow error that affects both
parts equally cancels out exactly. Two parts off one plate with one profile do
share most of their flow error.

**Spin time: predicted, NOT measured, NOT a claim, and NOT good.**
**{t_nom:.1} s** from a {rpm:.0} rpm launch, band {t_pes:.1}–{t_opt:.1} s.

I am not going to dress this up. A geared spinner cannot beat a plain one —
the gears add drag and buy almost no inertia — and a spinner with no bearing
cannot beat one with a bearing. Both of those are true here at once. The same
solver, on the same rotor, in the same run, says:

| build | non-printed parts | predicted spin |
|---|---|---|
| v1 — sliding ring land + one 608 | 1 | {t_slide:.1} s |
| v2 — ball thrust race + one 608 | 25 | {t_race:.1} s |
| **v3 — this model, fully printed** | **0** | **{t_nom:.1} s** |

**{cost:.1}× shorter than v2, and what it buys is that you need nothing.** No
sourcing, no waiting, no loose steel balls on the carpet, and nothing small
enough to swallow that you did not print yourself.

Where the time goes: **{coul:.0}% of the drag is constant-force (Coulomb)
friction**, which is the worst kind — the physics says a constant loss costs
~0.23× your spin time where an equal aerodynamic loss costs only ~0.76×. The
single biggest term is the ring: in a grounded-carrier star the ring is not on
a bearing (it is located by its six gear meshes), so held flat its own weight
rests on the frame and rubs, at r {r_ring:.1} mm. That is {ring_pct:.0}% of
everything. The sun's own thrust land, at r {r_sun:.1} mm, is another
{sun_pct:.0}%.

Both were attacked as hard as the geometry allows — the post shrank from Ø7.90
to Ø5.50 once it no longer had to be a bearing bore, and the ring pads moved in
to the last millimetre of flat underside the ring has. Everything else that
could help was costed and refused with numbers, including printed balls (they
would work mechanically; but a printed sphere is 167× further from round than a
bearing ball and nobody can model how something that lumpy rolls) and a central
web to the axis (no sun thickness anywhere in the design space leaves a
printable ring face). It is all in the analysis file, losers included.

**Hold it EDGE-ON for the longest spin — and yes, this advice has now changed
twice.** v1 said edge-on helped. v2 fitted a steel ball race under the ring and
flat became the fast way. v3 has no rolling element anywhere, so edge-on wins
again: {t_edge:.1} s against {t_nom:.1} s flat, **{ratio_edge:.1}×**. Not "the
friction vanishes" — the load moves into the meshes and out through the six
planet pin journals, which turn 5.5× faster than the ring. It does not leave
the machine.

**Break it in before you judge it.** Everything that slides here is printed PLA
on printed PLA, and printed plain bearings are documented to need a break-in.
Spin it by hand for a minute with light pressure in a few directions. That
effect is deliberately NOT in the prediction above, so it can only make your
number better than mine.

If you time it, please publish the protocol, not just a number: at least 5
runs, a repeatable release at {w0:.0} rad/s (not a hand flick — spin time
saturates with launch speed anyway), a stated stop criterion, and the spread
rather than the mean. There is currently exactly one methoded spin-time
measurement in this entire category and it is in a physics paper, not on a
model page.

**Not done, and it would be dishonest not to say so:** no drop/impact analysis
(PLA's notched toughness is low and this is a thing you drop; no printed-PLA
impact model exists in my toolchain); **no measurement of PLA-on-PLA friction at
a printed thrust face**, which is now the single load-bearing unknown in the
whole spin-time answer — all published data is PLA-on-steel at the wrong load,
which is exactly why the band is as wide as it is; no wear model for those same
faces; and no acoustic measurement.

### Print settings

- PLA, 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.20 mm layers
- 3 perimeters — **5 on the ring**, it is the part most likely to warp out of
  round and it is located by six meshes at once, so ovality binds everywhere
- 20% gyroid, **symmetric infill only** (a balance claim is meaningless if the
  slicer breaks the symmetry)
- **No supports. No brim** — every bed-touching edge of every moving part is
  relieved 0.45 mm radially on a 1.4:1 slope (deliberately steeper than 45°, so
  it is clear of the support threshold rather than sitting on it). That is both
  the brim replacement and the elephant-foot fix. Slicers remove between
  0.075 mm and 0.20 mm per side on layer 1 depending on vendor; the chamfer
  makes layer 1 physically absent from the running gaps so neither number can
  weld them shut.
- Turn ON avoid-crossing-walls / combing so no travel move crosses a clearance
  gap. Stringing inside a 0.25 mm gap welds it as surely as over-extrusion.
- Paint seams away from every running fit — and in this version that includes
  the post and the sun's bore, which are now a *bearing* rather than a press fit.
- Everything prints FLAT, gear axes vertical, on one plate, in one colour. No
  part in the set has a single downward-facing horizontal face, so there is
  nothing to bridge anywhere.

**Print `coupon_fit` and `coupon_key` first — about 12 minutes for both** and
they carry the three fits that decide the build. Every one is
printed-part-to-printed-part, because there is nothing to buy: a Ø5.50 journal
pin (the post and the planet pins are the same diameter, so one pin gauges
both — a printed planet must drop on and spin free), a Ø5.50 press boss for the
cap, and the **bayonet joint itself** — drop `coupon_key` over the third pin's
fin and slide it home. It should slide with no force and then refuse to lift.
If something is off, fix it there instead of on a 90-minute print.

If your planets are tight, the `optional/` folder has the same planet at Ø5.90
and Ø6.15 bores. The whole ladder costs about 2.5 g. Designed radial clearance
is 0.25 mm; on a printer running 0.15 mm oversize per side that closes to
nothing, and the Ø6.15 planet is the escape hatch.

### Assembly

Six steps, no hardware, no glue, no drilling, no tools:

1. Drop the sun over the post. Nothing to press — it turns directly on the
   printed post and rests on the small raised land at its base. It is located
   by its six gear meshes, not by the post, so a little rattle there is correct.
2. Drop six planets onto six pins. They are identical and cannot be installed
   wrong — the tooth counts are chosen so every planet position is
   mesh-equivalent, so each one self-clocks as it seats.
3. Drop the ring over the planets. It self-clocks too, and rests on the six
   thrust pads in the base rim.
4. Top spider on — the bayonet. Set it down about 7 deg anticlockwise of the
   base arms so each pin's fin drops through the wide end of its slot; it seats
   flat with no force. Then twist it 7 deg clockwise until all six stop.
5. Look at it. Every pin's fin must sit at the CLOSED end of its slot. That is
   the whole inspection, and it is the whole retention: {engage:.2} mm of solid
   pin over the slot wall, not a press fit your printer has to get right.
6. Press the cap on. It is the thumb pad and it also keeps the sun in when you
   turn the spinner over. This one IS an interference fit — the only one left.

**To run the A/B:** lift the cap, twist the top spider 7 deg back and lift it,
swap the sun for the control puck, re-fit. Twenty seconds.

### Safety

- **Not for children under 3** — the cap is a small part, and so is a planet if
  the top spider is lifted. Note that there is nothing in this box you did not
  print yourself: no loose steel balls, no bearing.
- The gear train is exposed by design. Every gap between moving parts is either
  under 5 mm (a finger cannot enter) or at least 12 mm (a finger is not
  crushed) — the {neighbour:.2} mm gap between adjacent planets is the only one
  a finger can reach into, and it clears the 12 mm branch. That is the EN 71-1
  §4.10 rod criterion, applied as a geometric check on the CAD. Note that the
  2026 edition of that standard has been published and I read the 2014 text, so
  treat this as a design criterion I held myself to, not a compliance
  certification.
- Every tooth tip is chamfered 0.30 mm on both faces.
- Keep solvents away from it. There is no bearing to de-grease any more, which
  was the one reason anybody ever put IPA near a printed spinner, and there are
  first-hand reports of printed spinner bodies shattering after a solvent clean.

---

## How this model was made

This model is **defined by a parametric program** — a Rust source file that
computes every dimension from first principles, builds the solids, and
re-verifies every claim on every run. It was not drawn by hand in a GUI CAD
package. Parametric, code-authored CAD is a long-established authoring method
(OpenSCAD, CadQuery, build123d) and the geometry here is the **deterministic
output of that program**: run it twice, get identical files.

**The program was written with AI assistance**, and so was the research that
froze its dimensions and its analysis plan. The geometry is not the output of a
generative 3-D model — there is no mesh generator anywhere in the pipeline. I
confirmed eligibility with Printables before entering.

Every number on this page is produced by the program's own run, not typed in by
hand:

- **110 gates** re-prove every claim on every build, and the build exits
  non-zero if any of them fails.
- Each gate that could silently pass has a **negative control** — a deliberately
  wrong version that must FAIL. The retention gate, for example, is falsified
  two ways: remove the lip and the spider must come off; pose it untwisted and
  it must not be captive.
- The honest limits are on the page too, not hidden: the predicted spin time,
  what has **not** been tested (drop and impact), and the one material constant
  nobody publishes for printed-PLA-on-printed-PLA, which is carried as a range
  rather than a value.

Full engineering write-up, including every design direction that was tried and
**rejected** with its numbers, is in the model's `analysis/` documentation.
